{"name":"High Reflective White","hex":"#f7f7f1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"high","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Physically elevated, extending above a base or average level:\n Very elevated; extending or being far above a base; tall; lofty.","Very elevated; extending or being far above a base; tall; lofty."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["In or to an elevated position.","In or at a great value."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/high"},{"term":"reflective","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That reflects, or redirects back to the source.","Pondering, especially thinking back on the past."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A supposed organ of the brain, dealing with abstract reasoning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/reflective"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Reflection (physics)","blocks":[{"text":"Reflection is the change in direction of a wavefront at an interface between two different media so that the wavefront returns into the medium from which it originated. Common examples include the reflection of light, sound and water waves. The law of reflection says that for specular reflection the angle at which the wave is incident on the surface equals the angle at which it is reflected."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Mount_Hood_reflected_in_Mirror_Lake%2C_Oregon.jpg/330px-Mount_Hood_reflected_in_Mirror_Lake%2C_Oregon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflection_(physics)","wikidata":"Q165939"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg/500px-MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg/500px-MIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sketches of specular, spread, diffuse, and compound reflection.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMIT_4.492_Daylighting%2C_Fall_2004_-_Types_of_Reflection_-_4815499473.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/500px-F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg/1280px-F%C3%A9nyvisszaver%C5%91d%C3%A9s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipe